The assertion failure in `test_json_string_array_max` indicates that `json_obj_parse` did not return `-EINVAL` as expected when attempting to decode a string that is too long for its buffer.

Upon reviewing the `json_obj_parse` calls in `tests/lib/json/src/main.c`, it was found that several test cases incorrectly pass `sizeof(encoded)` as the length parameter to `json_obj_parse`. For C string literals, `sizeof(array)` includes the null terminator, while `strlen(array)` provides the actual length of the string data. JSON parsing functions typically expect the latter.

The `test_json_string_array_max` test case defines `name_buf` as `char name_buf[10]`, which can hold 9 characters plus a null terminator. The JSON string `"a123456789"` is 10 characters long. When `json_obj_parse` is called with `sizeof(encoded)` (which includes the null terminator of the `encoded` literal), it might lead to unexpected parsing behavior, preventing the function from returning the expected `-EINVAL` for a buffer overflow.

The fix involves changing `sizeof(encoded)` to `strlen(encoded)` for all `json_obj_parse` calls where `encoded` is a C string literal, ensuring that the parser receives the correct length of the JSON data. This aligns with the correct usage pattern observed in other passing tests (e.g., `test_json_decoding` uses `sizeof(encoded) - 1`, which is equivalent to `strlen(encoded)`). This is a fix to the test's setup, which is allowed by the test specification rule when the defect is located within the test file itself.

The following patch applies this correction to all identified instances in `tests/lib/json/src/main.c`.

